fully discriminated, especially in the older English, the nominative and vocative being ''ye'' and the dative and accusative ''you''.
{{smaller block|<poem>He swor formest [first]
That ''ȝe'' schuld haue no harm, but hendely for gode
He praide ''ȝou'' com speke with him.</poem>
{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 269.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>He ȝaue ''ȝow'' fyue wittes
For to worshepen hym ther-with while ''ȝe'' lyuen here.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), ii. 15.}}}}
{{smaller block|And he said unto the elders, Tarry ''ye'' here for us, until we come again unto ''you''. {{right|Ex. xxiv. 14.}}}}
{{smaller block|Wherefore, brethren, look ''ye'' out among ''you'' seven men of honest report. {{right|Acts vi. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Yee'' Mannians, arme your selues, for feare of afterclaps.
{{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', I. 16.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Speed, Pegasus!—''ye'' strains of great and small,
Ode, epic, elegy, have at ''you'' all!</poem>
{{right|''Byron'', Eng. Bards and Scotch Reviewers.}}}}
(''b'') As used without discrimination of case-form between nominative and objective.
{{smaller block|<poem>''Ye'' a great master are in your degree.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', Mother Hub. Tale, l. 546.}}}}
{{smaller block|''You'' lie, ''ye'' rogue. {{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., ii. 2. 59.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The more shame for ''ye'', holy men I thought ''ye''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. VIII., iii. 1. 102.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''You'' meaner beauties of the night, . . .
What are ''you'' when the moon shall rise?</poem>
{{right|''Sir H. Wotton'', To the Queen of Bohemia.}}}}
{{smaller block|They have, like good sumpters, laid ''ye'' down their hors load of citations and fathers at your dore.
{{right|''Milton'', Church-Government, ii., Int.}}}}

