{{smaller block|{{right|''Tennyson'', Gareth and Lynette.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To you.''' See ''to''<sup>1</sup>.—'''You’re another,''' a familiar form of the tu quoque argument. See ''tu quoque.''}}
{{smaller block|I find little to interest and less to edify me in these international bandyings of “''You’re another.''”
{{right|''Lowell'', Democracy.}}}}

{{smaller|'''You-uns''' (literally, you ones), you. Compare ''we-uns'', under ''we.'' [Dialectal, southern U. S.]}}
{{smaller block|“Mirandy Jane,” the old woman interrupted, . . . “’pears like I hev hed the trouble o’ raisin’ a idjit in ''you-uns!''”
{{right|''M. N. Murfree'', Prophet of the Great Smoky Mountains, i.}}
<poem>But I’ll tell the yarn to ''youans.''</poem>
{{right|''John Hay'', Mystery of Gilgal.}}}}

'''ye<sup>2</sup>†''', ''adv.'' A Middle English form of ''yea.''

'''ye<sup>3</sup>†''', ''n.'' An obsolete variant of ''eye''<sup>1</sup>.

'''yea''' (yā), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''ye, ȝe, yai, yo,'' < AS. ''geá'' = OS. ''ja'' = OFries. ''iē, gē'' = D. ''ja'' = LG. ''ja'' = OHG. MHG. ''jā'', G. ''ja'' = Icel. ''jā'' = Dan. Sw. ''ja'' = Goth. ''ja'', yes, ''jai'', truly, verily; perhaps = Lith. ''ja'' in ''ja sakyti'', say yes, and Gr. ἦ, truly. Connection with AS. ''ge'' = Goth. ''jah'', also, and, and with L. ''jam'', now, Skt. ''ya'', who, is uncertain. Hence ult. ''yes.''] '''1.''' Yes; ay: a word that expresses affirmation or assent: the opposite of ''nay'': as, Will you go? ''Yea.''
{{smaller block|Swear not at all; . . . but let your communication be ''Yea, yea''; Nay, nay.{{right|Mat. v. 37.}}
You promise to bear Faith and Loyalty to him: Say ''Yea.'' And King Edward said ''Yea'', and kissed the King of France on the Mouth, as Lord of the Fee.
{{right|''Baker'', Chronicles, p. 117.}}}}
'''2.''' Indeed; verily; truly; it is so, or is it so? used to introduce a subject.
{{smaller block|''Yea'', hath God said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?{{right|Gen. iii. 1.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|''Yea'', mistress, are you so peremptory?
{{right|''Shak.'', Pericles, ii. 5. 73.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Him I loved not. Why?
I deem’d him fool? ''yea'', so?</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Pelleas and Ettarre.}}}}
'''3.''' Used to intimate that something is to be added by way of intensiveness or amplification: Not this alone; not only so but also; what is more. Compare the similar use of ''nay''.
{{smaller block|Confess Christ and his truth, not only in heart, but also in tongue, ''yea'', in very deed, which few gospellers do.
{{right|''J. Bradford'', Letters (Parker Soc., 1853), II. 202.}}}}
{{smaller block|I therein do rejoice; ''yea'', and will rejoice. Phil. i. 18.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>One that composed your beauties, ''yea'', and one
To whom you are but as a form in wax.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., i. 1. 48.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Many of you, ''yea'' most,
Return no more. ''Tennyson'', Holy Grail.</poem>}}
'''4.''' In the authorized version of the Bible, so; thus; true; real; consistent.
{{smaller block|All the promises of God in him are ''yea'', and in him Amen. 2 Cor. i. 20.}}
{{smaller block|''Yea'' is now used only in the sacred, solemn, or formal style. ''Yea'', being mainly a word of assent, was formerly used chiefly in answer to questions framed affirmatively; ''yes'', a stronger term, was chiefly used in answer to questions containing a negative or otherwise implying a doubt. But the distinction does not appear to have been rigidly maintained; and the assertions of the following quotations about ''yea'' and ''yes'', like those about ''nay'' and ''no'' (see ''no''<sup>1</sup>), must be taken with some allowance.}}
