'''yean''' (yēn), ''v. t.'' and ''i.'' [< ME. *''ȝenen'', *''ȝe-enen'', < AS. *''ge-eánian'', ''ge-eácnian'', bring forth, become pregnant, < ''eácen'', ''ge-eácen'', gravid, teeming: see ''ean''.] To bring forth young, as a goat or sheep; lamb.
{{smaller block|That wherein the courteous man takes most sauour is . . . to sell his wine deare, . . . his eawes to haue good ''yeaning'', not to raine in April, and to haue much wheate in Maie. ''Guevara'', Letters (tr. by Hellowes, 1577), p. 254.
<poem>So many weeks ere the poor fools will ''yean''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI. (ed. Knight), ii. 5. 36.}}
<poem>Yon ’s one hath ''yean’d'' a fearful prodigy,
Some monstrous misshapen balladry.</poem>
{{right|''Marston'', Scourge of Villanie, vi. 39.}}
<poem>Weak as a lamb the hour that it is ''yeaned''.</poem>
{{right|''Wordsworth'', Hart-Leap Well.}}
<poem>Trenchant time behoves to hurry
All to ''yean'' and all to bury.</poem>
{{right|''Emerson'', Wood-notes, ii.}}}}

'''yeanling''' (yēn′ling), ''n.'' [< ''yean'' + ''-ling''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''eanling''.] The young of sheep or goats; a lamb; a kid; an eanling: sometimes used attributively.
{{smaller block|<poem>To their store
They add the poor man’s ''yeanling'', and dare sell
Both fleece and carcass, not gi’ing him the fell!</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Sad Shepherd, i. 2.}}
Lambs, or ''yeanling'' kids. ''Milton'', P. L., iii. 434.}}

'''year''' (yēr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''yeer'', ''yer'', ''ȝer'', < AS. ''geár'' ''gēr'' (pl. ''geár'') = OS. ''jār'', ''gēr'' = OFries. ''jār'', ''jēr'' = MD. ''jaer'', D. ''jaar'', ''jār'' = LG. ''jaar'' = OHG. MHG. ''jār'', G. ''jahr'' = Icel. ''ār'' = Sw. ''år'' = Dan. ''aar'' = Goth. ''jēr'', year; prob. orig. ‘spring,’ the opening of the year, = OBulg. ''jarŭ'', spring. = Gr.
